$DIR/main.p:20:10: cannot pass &n to call, which keeps it after returning
    &n is a pointer to n. n is a local variable of main (declared at main.p:19).
    call passes v to s.String (which may call main.(*Named).String) (main.p:14),
    which stores n in package variable last (main.p:7), so &n would outlive n.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If call should keep its own copy of n, change it to take the value, not a
    pointer, or an owner it can keep: an owned[Named] from new, a shared[Named],
    or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:20:20: cannot pass Wide{...} to call, which keeps it after returning
    Converting a value larger than one word to an interface puts a copy of the
    value in the frame of the function that converts it (here main, at
    main.p:20), and the interface points at that copy. call passes v to s.String
    (which may call main.(*Named).String) (main.p:14), which stores n in package
    variable last (main.p:7), so the interface would outlive main's frame.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Change call to take a value or an owner, which it can keep: an owned[T] from
    new, a shared[T], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
